WEBVTT

00:00:00.825 --> 00:00:04.025
Listener: Why is the refund for order one
oh four stuck?

00:00:04.725 --> 00:00:09.575
Guide: The refund job called the payment
API and timed out waiting for a reply.

00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:11.900
Its call record tells us that much.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.575
To trace the request, start with what each
system recorded.

00:00:15.575 --> 00:00:17.600
Kubernetes lists workloads,

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.625
a network flow names two endpoints, and a
trace shows a call.

00:00:21.625 --> 00:00:23.775
Each dot is one source record.

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:29.000
Listener: What tells us that two records
describe the same thing?

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:33.475
Guide: These two carry the same full Azure
resource ID.

00:00:33.475 --> 00:00:36.475
Azure Resource Graph lists a storage
account.

00:00:36.475 --> 00:00:39.825
Azure Monitor puts that account’s ID on a
reading.

00:00:39.825 --> 00:00:43.075
Matching the ID ties the reading to the
account.

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:45.925
The DNS record supplies a different link.

00:00:45.925 --> 00:00:48.000
Its name resolves to an address,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.700
and the private endpoint at that address
names the storage account.

00:00:53.317 --> 00:00:56.567
Listener: Can I see how the technical
parts fit together?

00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:57.892
Guide: Yes.

00:00:57.892 --> 00:01:01.967
The DNS answer leads to a private endpoint
and then to storage.

00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:04.442
Follow that path as the dots move.

00:01:04.442 --> 00:01:07.467
This projection groups records by
technology.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:12.142
Kubernetes workloads sit together,
alongside groups for Azure resources,

00:01:12.142 --> 00:01:17.092
network connections, DNS, application
telemetry and shop software.

00:01:17.092 --> 00:01:19.017
The links still cross the groups.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.125
Listener: Where does that leave the refund
job?

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.550
Guide: It belongs to Payout.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:30.400
It reads the case store in Returns Intake
and calls the payment API.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.350
Grouping by capability puts software with
the same job together.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.875
Its original links explain what each group
depends on.

00:01:37.875 --> 00:01:40.100
Shared infrastructure has its own group,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.500
so the path to storage is still there.

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:47.383
Listener: Which view would help me
investigate the timeout?

00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:51.833
Guide: The dashboard shows successful
payment calls falling to zero.

00:01:52.363 --> 00:01:54.913
The health tree shows why that blocks
Payout.

00:01:55.443 --> 00:01:59.518
The Kubernetes view follows the refund
deployment to its pods.

00:02:00.048 --> 00:02:03.098
The Azure resource view lists the storage
account.

00:02:03.628 --> 00:02:08.253
The DNS and IP view traces its name to a
private address.

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:11.328
Each projection draws on the same shop
evidence

00:02:11.328 --> 00:02:13.178
to answer a different question.

00:02:15.217 --> 00:02:19.592
Listener: If the database stops answering,
why does web turn red too?

00:02:20.342 --> 00:02:24.817
Guide: Web needs the API, and the API
needs the database.

00:02:24.817 --> 00:02:27.592
A health roll-up lets a problem in a
dependency

00:02:27.592 --> 00:02:30.267
change the state shown for its callers.

00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:33.117
Follow the recorded pg-shop outage.

00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:38.267
The database signal turns red, and web
inherits that state through the API.

00:02:39.925 --> 00:02:43.000
Listener: What makes the database red in
the first place?

00:02:43.725 --> 00:02:47.400
Guide: Its availability meter reads zero
at ten eleven.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.750
Azure Monitor reports that sample a minute
later.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:55.000
The shop’s database rule opens an alarm
after one bad minute.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.125
The reading is what the meter saw.

00:02:57.125 --> 00:02:59.525
The rule decides what it means for health.

00:03:01.175 --> 00:03:04.725
Listener: How does Sattle work out which
services feel that alarm?

00:03:05.425 --> 00:03:09.300
Guide: The structure query records which
parts can affect each service.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.025
The health query starts at the service,
follows up

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:16.650
to sixteen links called Affects, and takes
the worst severity it finds.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.575
Web reaches the database along that path.

00:03:19.575 --> 00:03:23.850
The cache has no such path to the failing
server, so it stays green.

00:03:25.483 --> 00:03:27.183
Listener: The meter is back at one.

00:03:27.183 --> 00:03:29.483
Why is the database still red?

00:03:30.208 --> 00:03:32.908
Guide: The rule waits for two good minutes
in a row.

00:03:32.908 --> 00:03:34.983
Ten fourteen is the first.

00:03:34.983 --> 00:03:39.033
Ten fifteen clears the alarm, and the
callers recover too.

00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:42.333
In the exported graph, revision sixteen
shows the failure

00:03:42.333 --> 00:03:44.333
and eighteen shows recovery.

00:03:44.333 --> 00:03:46.658
Those numbers count graph changes.

00:03:46.658 --> 00:03:48.633
The meter supplies the times.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.725
Listener: Suppose an API pod starts
sending traffic to Redis.

00:03:54.725 --> 00:03:56.650
What does Sattle see first?

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.150
Guide: One new Hubble flow.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:03.650
In a copy of the pg-shop recording, an API
pod sends traffic

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.425
to the address of an existing Redis cache.

00:04:06.425 --> 00:04:10.550
The master graph gains one link from the
pod to the destination.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.450
Both were already on the map.

00:04:14.092 --> 00:04:18.717
Listener: How does that address become a
Redis dependency in the health model?

00:04:19.417 --> 00:04:23.217
Guide: Kubernetes shows that the pod backs
the API service.

00:04:23.217 --> 00:04:27.317
The destination address matches a private
endpoint for the Redis cache.

00:04:27.317 --> 00:04:30.092
The host name identifies the same cache.

00:04:30.092 --> 00:04:35.317
The existing structure query uses that
evidence to add an API dependency.

00:04:35.317 --> 00:04:36.642
During the cache warning,

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:41.242
the health model now marks both the API
and its gateway Degraded.

00:04:42.883 --> 00:04:44.883
Listener: Where can I see what was new?

00:04:45.633 --> 00:04:48.458
Guide: Look at the revision that adds the
dependency.

00:04:48.458 --> 00:04:52.058
Its patch lists a new link from the API to
Redis.

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:55.983
Compare the two revisions in the Canvas to
highlight what changed.

00:04:55.983 --> 00:04:58.033
Redis was already on the map.

00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:00.833
The new part is the API’s dependency,

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:04.108
and the existing health rule already knows
how to use it.

00:05:05.758 --> 00:05:10.183
Listener: How could I check that the API
really started using that cache?

00:05:10.883 --> 00:05:14.008
Guide: The structure query kept two pieces
of evidence.

00:05:14.008 --> 00:05:18.408
One names the pod, its destination address
and the private endpoint.

00:05:18.408 --> 00:05:20.433
The other names the Redis host.

00:05:20.433 --> 00:05:23.883
You can check both against the flow and
Azure records.

00:05:23.883 --> 00:05:26.583
The traffic explains the new dependency.

00:05:26.583 --> 00:05:29.758
The cache’s own load readings explain the
warning.
